    <h1>🐴 Horse Care</h1>
    <p>Taking care of a horse is a big responsibility — but also incredibly rewarding. A well-cared-for horse is healthy, happy, and trusting. This page covers the key areas every horse owner should know.</p>

    <h2>🍽️ Nutrition</h2>
    <p>Horses are herbivores that need a consistent, balanced diet. The foundation of any horse's diet is forage — mainly hay and grass. A typical adult horse eats about 1.5–2% of its body weight in forage per day.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Fresh hay and grass are the main food source</li>
        <li>Grains may be added for working horses</li>
        <li>Fresh, clean water must always be available (30–50 liters per day)</li>
        <li>Avoid giving bread, chocolate, or processed foods</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="HorseNutritionOverview.aspx">➡️ Read more about horse nutrition</a></p>

    <h2>🧼 Grooming</h2>
    <p>Regular grooming keeps a horse's coat clean and healthy, and is also a great bonding activity. It should be done before and after riding.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Brush the body with a body brush and curry comb daily</li>
        <li>Use a mane and tail comb to detangle carefully</li>
        <li>Clean hooves with a hoof pick before every ride</li>
        <li>Bathe the horse in warm weather using horse shampoo</li>
        <li>Check skin for irritations, cuts, or parasites during grooming</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🩺 Health &amp; Vet Care</h2>
    <p>Preventive healthcare is essential for keeping your horse in top condition.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Schedule vet checkups at least once or twice a year</li>
        <li>Vaccinate against flu, tetanus, and other regional diseases</li>
        <li>Deworm your horse every 6–12 months</li>
        <li>Have a farrier trim or shoe hooves every 6–8 weeks</li>
        <li>Watch for signs of illness: loss of appetite, lethargy, or limping</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="HorseHealth.aspx">➡️ Read more about horse health</a></p>

    <h2>🏠 Shelter &amp; Living Space</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>A stable should be well-ventilated and dry</li>
        <li>Stall size should be at least 3.5m x 3.5m for a standard horse</li>
        <li>Bedding (straw or wood shavings) should be changed regularly</li>
        <li>Horses also need daily turnout time in a paddock or field</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>💞 Bonding</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Spend time with your horse every day, not just during riding</li>
        <li>Speak in a calm, low voice</li>
        <li>Use positive reinforcement — treats and praise after good behavior</li>
        <li>Learn to read your horse's body language</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="BondingWithHorse.aspx">➡️ Read more about bonding with your horse</a></p>

